<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-applying-the-quadratic-formula" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>Applying the Quadratic Formula</title>
    <objectives component="outcomes">
        <introduction>
            <p>
            <dl>
                <li>
                    <title>Strand</title>
                    <p>
                        1.0 Numbers and Algebra
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>Sub-Strand</title>
                    <p>
                        1.1 Quadratic Expressions and Equations
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>Specific Learning Outcomes</title> 
                    <p>
                        Solve quadratic equations using the quadratic formula
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li component="lesson-plan">
                    <title>Grade Level</title>
                    <p>
                        11
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li component="lesson-plan">
                    <title>Estimated Duration</title>
                    <p>
                        40 minutes
                    </p>
                </li>
            </dl>
            </p>
        </introduction>
    </objectives>

    <!-- [LP] Add guidance on activity -->

    <axiom component="lesson-plan">
            <title>Problem-Solving and Discovery</title>
            <dl>
                <li>
                    <title>Suggested Time</title>
                    <p>
                        15 minutes
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>Objective</title>
                    <p>
                        To explore how quadratic equations are formed through operations on a secret variable and solve them by applying the quadratic formula to "uncover" the original value.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>Teacher's Role</title>
                    <p>
                        Facilitate the group activity by ensuring roles are clearly assigned and constraints are respected. Guide learners in translating the "Announcer's" result into a quadratic equation of the form <m>x(x+5) = k</m>.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </dl>
        </axiom>

    <activity>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                <alert>Work in Groups</alert>
            </p>
            <p>
                Form groups of <m>4</m> and give each member one of the following roles:
            </p>
            <p>
                <ul>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Person 1 (the Chooser): chooses a secret number <m>x</m>.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Person 2 (the Adder): adds <m>5</m> to the secret number to get <m>x + 5</m>.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Person 3 (the Multiplier): multiplies the secret number by Person 2's result to get <m>x(x + 5)</m>.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Person 4 (the Announcer): announces the final product to the rest of the class.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </p>
            <p>
                The rest of the class then works out the secret number <m>x</m> from the announced product alone.
            </p>
            <p>
                <alert>What to do</alert>
            </p>
            <p>
                <ul>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Person <m>1</m> chooses a secret number <m>x</m> and whispers it to Persons <m>2</m> and <m>3</m> only.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Person <m>2</m> adds <m>5</m> to the secret number and whispers the result, <m>x + 5</m>, to Person <m>3</m>.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Person <m>3</m> multiplies the secret number <m>x</m> by <m>x + 5</m> and whispers the product <m>x(x + 5)</m> to Person <m>4</m>.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Person <m>4</m> announces the product.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </p>

        </introduction>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    If Person <m>4</m> says the product is <m>14</m>, guess the secret number chosen by Person <m>1</m>. Is there more than one possible answer?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    What if Person <m>4</m> says the product is <m>13.5</m>?
                </p>
                <p>
                    Can the secret number be determined by guessing alone? Explain why or why not.
                </p>
                <p>
                    How will you find the secret number in this case? Give the value of <m>x</m> that satisfies the equation.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Repeat the activity with Person <m>2</m> adding a different number, so that Person <m>4</m> announces a new product.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    <ul>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                 What method did you use to solve the equations?
                            </p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                 What are the advantages of using the quadratic formula compared to other methods of solving quadratic equations?
                            </p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                Can you think of any real-life situations where applying the quadratic formula would be useful?
                            </p>
                        </li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Share your work with the other learners in the class.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
    </activity> 

    <!-- [LP] Add guidance on structured lesson plan-->

    <axiom component="lesson-plan">
        <title>Structured Instruction</title>
        <p>
            <dl>
            <li>
                <title>Suggested Time</title>
                <p>
                    10 minutes
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <title>Objective</title>
                <p>
                    To formalize the application of the quadratic formula and introduce the role of the discriminant in determining the nature of roots.
                </p>
            </li>
            </dl>
        </p>
        <paragraphs>
            <title>Key Takeaways &amp; Teacher Connection</title>
            <ol>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        <alert>Connecting to Learner Work:</alert> Refer to the activity where "guessing" became difficult with decimals like 13.5. Explain that the quadratic formula provides a systematic way to solve such equations without guessing. For a product of <m>13.5</m>, learners solve <m>x^2 + 5x - 13.5 = 0</m> to get <m>x = \frac{-5 \pm \sqrt{79}}{2}</m>, that is, <m>x \approx 1.94</m> or <m>x \approx -6.94</m>.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        <alert>Two answers:</alert> Point out that a product of <m>14</m> comes from <m>x = 2</m> <em>and</em> from <m>x = -7</m>. A quadratic equation can have two solutions, so learners should look for both.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        <alert>The discriminant:</alert> Show how the value of <m>b^2 - 4ac</m> tells us, before solving, whether the equation has two real solutions, one repeated solution, or no real solutions.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ol>
        </paragraphs>
    </axiom>

    <!-- Add insight / key takeaway directly related to activity / learner experience -->

     <insight>
        <p>
            The quadratic formula is a universal method for solving quadratic equations of the form <m>ax^2 + bx + c = 0</m>
            , particularly when factoring is difficult or impossible. 
            
        </p>
        <p>
            The formula is given by:
            <m>x = \frac{-b \pm \sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a}</m>
        </p>
        <p>
            where <m>a</m>, <m>b</m>, and <m>c</m> are coefficients from the quadratic equation.
        </p>
        <p>
            The discriminant, <m>b^2 - 4ac</m>, determines the nature of the roots:
            <ul>
                <li>
                    If the discriminant is positive, there are two distinct real roots.
                </li>
                <li>
                    If the discriminant is zero, there is exactly one real root (a repeated root).
                </li>
                <li>
                    If the discriminant is negative, there are no real roots, but two complex conjugate roots.
                </li>
            </ul>
        </p>
        <p>
            <alert>How to apply quadratic formula</alert>
        </p>
        <p>
            <ol>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Arrange all terms to one side to set the equation to zero: <m>ax^2 + bx + c = 0</m>.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Identify the values of the coefficients <m>a</m>, <m>b</m>, and <m>c</m> from the quadratic equation.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Substitute the values of <m>a</m>, <m>b</m>, and <m>c</m> into the quadratic formula.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Solve for <m>x</m> by simplifying the expression under the square root, ensuring to calculate both the plus and minus scenarios
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ol>
        </p>
    </insight> 

    <!-- Add further instruction (not in a block) to develop ideas as needed -->

    <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/example-intro.ptx" />

    <!-- Add at least 3 examples -->

    <example>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Solve the quadratic equation <m>2x^2 - 4x - 6 = 0</m> using the quadratic formula.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                First, identify the coefficients: <m>a = 2</m>, <m>b = -4</m>, and <m>c = -6</m>.
            </p>
            <p>
                Next, substitute these values into the quadratic formula:
            </p>
            <p>
                <m>
                    x = \frac{-(-4) \pm \sqrt{(-4)^2 - 4(2)(-6)}}{2(2)}
                </m>
            </p>
            <p>
                Simplify the expression under the square root:
            </p>
            <p>
                <m>
                    x = \frac{4 \pm \sqrt{16 + 48}}{4}
                </m>
            </p>
            <p>
                <m>
                    x = \frac{4 \pm \sqrt{64}}{4}
                </m>
            </p>
            <p>
                <m>
                    x = \frac{4 \pm 8}{4}
                </m>
            </p>
            <p>
                This gives us two solutions:
            </p>
            <p>
                <m>
                    x = \frac{4 + 8}{4} = 3
                </m>
            </p>
            <p>
                and
            </p>
            <p> 
                <m>
                    x = \frac{4 - 8}{4} = -1
                </m>
            </p>

        </solution>
    </example>

    <example>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Solve: 
                <m>
                    x^2 + 6x + 5 = 0                               
                </m>
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                Identify the coefficients: <m>a = 1</m>, <m>b = 6</m>, and <m>c = 5</m>.
            </p>
            <p>
                Substitute into the quadratic formula:
            </p>
            <p>
                <m>
                    x = \frac{-6 \pm \sqrt{6^2 - 4(1)(5)}}{2(1)}
                </m>
            </p>
            <p>
                Simplify the expression under the square root:
            </p>
            <p>
                <md>
                    <mrow> x \amp = \frac{-6 \pm \sqrt{36 - 20}}{2}</mrow>
                    <mrow> x =\amp \frac{-6 \pm \sqrt{16}}{2}</mrow>
                    <mrow> x =\amp \frac{-6 \pm 4}{2}</mrow>
                    <mrow> x =\amp \frac{-2}{2} \quad \text{or} \quad x = \frac{-10}{2}</mrow>
                </md>
            </p>  
            <p>
                Therefore, the solutions are: <m>x= -1</m> or <m>x = -5</m>.
            </p>   
        </solution>
    </example>

    <example>
        <statement>
            <p>
                A ball is thrown upward from <m>20</m> meters high with an initial velocity of <m>15</m> m/s. Its height <m>h</m>  after <m>t</m>
                 seconds is <m>h= -5t^2 + 15t + 20</m> . After how many seconds does the ball hit the ground? 

            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                To find when the ball hits the ground, we set <m>h = 0</m> and solve for <m>t</m>:
            </p>
            <p>
                <m>
                    -5t^2 + 15t + 20 = 0
                </m>
            </p>
            <p>
                Divide the entire equation by 5 to simplify:
            </p>
            <p>
                <m>
                    t^2 - 3t - 4 = 0
                </m>
            </p>
            <p>
                Identify the coefficients: <m>a = 1</m>, <m>b = -3</m>, and <m>c = -4</m>.
            </p>
            <p>
                Substitute into the quadratic formula:
            </p>
            <p>
                <m>
                    t = \frac{-(-3) \pm \sqrt{(-3)^2 - 4(1)(-4)}}{2(1)}
                </m>
            </p>
            <p>
                Simplify the expression under the square root:
            </p>
            <p>
                <m>
                    t = \frac{3 \pm \sqrt{9 + 16}}{2}
                </m>
            </p>
            <p>
                <m>
                    t = \frac{3 \pm \sqrt{25}}{2}
                </m>
            </p>
            <p>
                Since the discriminant is positive, there are two real solutions:
            </p>
            <p>
                <m>
                    t = \frac{3 + 5}{2} = 4
                </m>
            </p>
            <p>
                and
            </p>
            <p> 
                <m>
                    t = \frac{3 - 5}{2} = -1
                </m>
            </p>
            <p>
                Since time cannot be negative, the ball hits the ground after <m>4</m> seconds.
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example> 

    <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/assessment-intro.ptx" />

    <!-- If STACK questions are included, uncomment this: -->

    <!-- <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/checkpoint.ptx" /> -->

    <!-- Add STACK exercises if made -->

    <!-- Add static exercises -->

    <exercise xml:id="ex-3-2-4-2-using-the-quadratic-formula">
        <!--<title></title>-->
        <stack label="stk-3-2-4-2-using-the-quadratic-formula" source="stack/top/1-numbers-and-algebra/01-quadratic-expressions-and-equations/05-applying-the-quadratic-formula/3-2-4-2-Using-the-Quadratic-Formula.xml"/>
    </exercise>
    <exercise xml:id="ex-3-2-4-2-possible-modification-using-the-quadratic-formula">
        <!--<title></title>-->
        <stack label="stk-3-2-4-2-possible-modification-using-the-quadratic-formula" source="stack/top/1-numbers-and-algebra/01-quadratic-expressions-and-equations/05-applying-the-quadratic-formula/3-2-4-2-Possible-modification-Using-the-Quadratic-Formula.xml"/>
    </exercise>

    <exercises>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Solve the following quadratic equations using the quadratic formula.
            </p>
        </introduction>
        <exercise>
            <statement>   
              <p>
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <m>x^2+5x+6 = 0</m>
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <m>x^2-7x+10 = 0</m>
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <m>2x^2 + 3x - 2 = 0</m>
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <m> 3x^2 - 5x - 2 = 0</m>
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
              </p>
            </statement>   
            <answer>
               <p>
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <m>x = -2 \text{ or } x = -3</m>
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <m>x = 2 \text{ or } x = 5</m>
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <m>x = -2 \text{ or } x = \frac{1}{2}</m>
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <m>x = -\frac{1}{3} \text{ or } x = 2</m>
                        </p>
                    </li>

                </ol>
               </p>
            </answer>             
        </exercise>  
        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Solve:
                </p>
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <m>x^2 - 2x - 1 = 0</m>
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <m>x^2 + 4x + 1 = 0</m>
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <m>2x^2 + x - 1 = 0</m>
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                           <m> 3x^2 - x - 1 = 0</m>
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>x = 1 + \sqrt{2} \text{ or } x = 1 - \sqrt{2}</m>
                            </p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>x = -2 + \sqrt{3} \text{ or } x = -2 - \sqrt{3}</m>
                            </p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>x = \frac{-1 \pm \sqrt{9}}{4} = \frac{-1 \pm 3}{4}</m>, so <m>x = \frac{1}{2}</m> or <m>x = -1</m>
                            </p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>x = \frac{1 + \sqrt{13}}{6} \text{ or } x = \frac{1 - \sqrt{13}}{6}</m>
                            </p>
                        </li>

                    </ol>
                </p>
            </answer>
        </exercise>  
        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Solve:
                </p>
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <m>x^2 - 6x + 9 = 0</m>
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <m>4x^2 - 4x + 1 = 0</m>
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>x = 3</m> (a repeated root)
                            </p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>x = \frac{1}{2}</m> (a repeated root)
                            </p>
                        </li>

                    </ol>
                </p>
            </answer>
        </exercise> 
        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Solve:
                </p>
                <p>
                    <ol>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>5x^2 + 2x - 3 = 0</m>
                            </p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>2x^2 + 3x + 4 = 0</m>
                            </p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>6x^2 + x - 2 = 0</m>
                            </p>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>x = \frac{-2 \pm \sqrt{64}}{10} = \frac{-2 \pm 8}{10}</m>, so <m>x = \frac{3}{5}</m> or <m>x = -1</m>
                            </p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                The discriminant is <m>3^2 - 4(2)(4) = -23</m>, which is negative, so the equation has no real solutions.
                            </p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>x = \frac{-1 \pm \sqrt{49}}{12} = \frac{-1 \pm 7}{12}</m>, so <m>x = \frac{1}{2}</m> or <m>x = -\frac{2}{3}</m>
                            </p>
                        </li>

                    </ol>
                </p>
            </answer>
        </exercise>  
        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    A school sells tickets for an event. The revenue <m>R</m> (in shillings) is given by the equation <m>R = -2x^2 + 100x</m>.
                </p>
                <p>
                    where x is the number of tickets sold (in tens).
                </p>
                <p>
                    Find the number of tickets sold when the revenue is <m>450</m> shillings.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                
                <p>
                    <m>
                        2x^2 - 100x + 450 = 0
                    </m>
                </p>
                <p>
                    <m>
                        x^2 - 50x + 225 = 0
                    </m>
                </p>
                <p>
                    <m>
                        x = \frac{-(-50) \pm \sqrt{(-50)^2 - 4(1)(225)}}{2(1)}
                    </m>
                </p>
                <p>
                    <m>
                        x = \frac{50 \pm \sqrt{1600}}{2} = \frac{50 \pm 40}{2} 
                    </m>
                </p>
                <p>
                    So <m>x = 45</m> or <m>x = 5</m>.
                </p>
                <p>
                    Since <m>x</m> is the number of tickets sold in tens, the revenue is <m>450</m> shillings when <m>50</m> tickets or <m>450</m> tickets are sold. Both answers are valid: the revenue rises to a maximum at <m>x = 25</m> (<m>250</m> tickets) and then falls again. 
                </p>
            </answer>
        </exercise> 
    </exercises> 

    <!-- [LP] Add information on how to differentiate  -->

    <axiom component="lesson-plan">
        <title>Differentiation</title>

        <table>
            <title>Differentiation</title>
            <tabular halign="left">
                <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
                    <cell>Learner Group</cell>
                    <cell>Strategy &amp; Learning Experience</cell>
                    <cell></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell>Struggling Learners (Support)</cell>
                    <cell>
                        Provide a substitution template to help identify <m>a, b, c</m> and guide them through the order of operations within the formula.
                    </cell>
                    <cell></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell>On-Level Learners (Core)</cell>
                    <cell>
                        <line>The core lesson activities as described above, solving various equations including those with irrational roots.</line>
                    </cell>
                    <cell></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell>Advanced Learners (Challenge)</cell>
                    <cell>
                        Investigate cases where the discriminant is negative and explain why no real solutions exist, linking it back to the graph of a quadratic function.
                    </cell>
                    <cell></cell>
                </row>
            </tabular>
        </table>
    </axiom>

